@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.30}Exercise 4.30:} Cy D. Fect, a reformed C
programmer, is worried that some side effects may never take place because the
lazy evaluator does not force the result of every expression in a sequence.
He proposes forcing each non-final expression result as well as evaluating
that expression.

In this edition the relevant control paths are
@code{Evaluator.execStatement}, @code{runBody}, and @code{blockValue}.
An @code{ExpressionStatement} evaluates its expression, then the sequence
continues; lazy mode inherits this control and overrides argument delay and
value exposure.  There is no separate @code{actualValue} helper or
@code{GExprStmt} node.  Treat Cy's proposal as a semantic alternative and use
the examples below to decide whether it changes observable output or state.

@enumerate a

@item
Ben Bitdiddle thinks Cy is wrong.  He shows Cy the @code{forEach} procedure
described in @ref{Exercise 2.23}, which gives an important example of a
sequence with side effects:

@example kotlin
fun forEach(proc: (Long) -> Unit, items: List<Long>) @{
    if (items.isEmpty()) @{
        return
    @}
    proc(items.get(0))
    forEach(proc, items.drop(1))
@}

fun main() @{
    forEach(
        @{ x ->
            println()
            println(x)
        @},
        listOf(57L, 321L, 88L),
    )
@}
@end example

Each call to @code{proc} emits a blank line followed by its element,
so the output is blank, @code{57}, blank, @code{321}, blank, and
@code{88}, in that order.  Explain why the lazy evaluator still executes
each effectful call as it advances through the body.

@item
Cy agrees that Ben is right about the @code{forEach} example, but says that
that's not the kind of program he was thinking about when he proposed his
change to sequence handling.  He defines the following two procedures in the
lazy evaluator:

@example kotlin
fun p1(init: Long): Long @{
    var x = init
    x = x + 1L
    return x
@}

fun p2(init: Long): Long @{
    var x = init
    fun p(e: Long): Long @{
        e
        return x
    @}
    fun bump(): Long @{
        x = x + 1L
        return x
    @}
    return p(bump())
@}

fun main() @{
    println(p1(1L))
    println(p2(1L))
@}
@end example

In this engine, both calls return 2.  In @code{p2}, evaluating the
non-final name @code{e} reaches @code{exposeValue}, which forces its
transparent thunk and runs @code{bump}.  Would an additional force of each
non-final expression change either result or effect?

@item
Would forcing each non-final result again change part a in this engine?
Explain how strict @code{println} arguments and name exposure determine the
