@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.31}Exercise 4.31:} The approach taken in this
section is somewhat unpleasant, because it makes an incompatible change to the
base language.  It might be nicer to implement lazy evaluation as an
@newterm{upward-compatible extension}, that is, so that ordinary core
guest programs will work as before.  We can do this with the parameter
annotations of the lazy mode, which let the user control whether or not
arguments are to be delayed.  For example, the definition

@example kotlin
fun f(@@Strict a: Long, b: Long, @@Strict c: Long,
    @@Delayed d: Long): Long =
    @r{…}
@end example

@noindent
would define @code{f} to be a procedure of four arguments, where the first and
third arguments are evaluated when the procedure is called and the second and
fourth arguments are delayed.  Thus, ordinary procedure definitions (all
parameters @code{@@Strict}) produce the same behavior as the base language,
while leaving every parameter of every compound procedure unannotated produces
the behavior of the lazy evaluator defined in this section.  In this edition
every delayed argument is memoized: a thunk computes at most once
(@ref{4.2.2}), so there is no non-memoized @code{lazy} spelling to implement.
Design and implement the changes required to produce such an extension.  You
will have to extend the admission of parameter annotations in function
declarations.  You must also arrange for application to determine when
arguments are to be delayed, and to force or delay arguments accordingly.
@end quotation

