@subsection Streams as Lazy Lists

In @ref{3.5.1}, stream tails were represented by delayed computations.
The guest @code{Lazy} mode admits @code{thunk}, @code{force},
@code{lazyPair}, and @code{lazyEnd}.  A lazy pair stores an available head
and a memoized thunk for its tail.  The source type is @code{List<T>}, but a
lazy pair has a distinct runtime representation from the eager @code{VList}
created by @code{listOf}.

An explicit thunk is a first-class guest value, so it can be passed through
the higher-order procedures that ordinary special forms cannot handle.
Compound-procedure arguments are delayed by the Lazy evaluator, while
primitive calls remain strict.  The representation used by this edition is
not the procedural pair encoding of @ref{Exercise 2.4}; it uses the named
lazy-list constructors:

@example kotlin
fun cons(x: Long, rest: List<Long>): List<Long> =
    lazyPair(x, thunk @{ rest @})

fun car(z: List<Long>): Long = z.get(0)

fun cdr(z: List<Long>): List<Long> = z.drop(1)
@end example

The following guest definitions illustrate the intended list operations.
They use the ordinary @code{List} selectors, so their behavior on
@code{VLazyList} depends on how those selectors are implemented:
@example kotlin
fun listRef(items: List<Long>, n: Long): Long =
    if (n == 0L) @{
        car(items)
    @} else @{
        listRef(cdr(items), n - 1L)
    @}

fun mapList(proc: (Long) -> Long,
    items: List<Long>): List<Long> =
    if (items.isEmpty()) @{
        lazyEnd
    @} else @{
        cons(proc(car(items)),
                mapList(proc,
            cdr(items)))
    @}

fun scaleList(items: List<Long>,
    factor: Long): List<Long> =
    mapList(@{ x -> x * factor @}, items)

fun addLists(list1: List<Long>,
    list2: List<Long>): List<Long> =
    if (list1.isEmpty()) @{
        list2
    @} else if (list2.isEmpty()) @{
        list1
    @} else @{
        cons(car(list1) + car(list2),
                addLists(cdr(list1),
            cdr(list2)))
    @}

fun ones(): List<Long> = cons(1L, ones())

fun integers(): List<Long> = cons(1L,
        addLists(ones(),
    integers()))
@end example

@example kotlin
fun main() @{
    println(listRef(integers(), 17L))
@}
@end example

@noindent
The Lazy module has specialized operations for lazy lists.  For a
@code{VLazyList}, @code{get} traverses only to the requested index,
@code{drop} forces only the skipped tail nodes, and @code{isEmpty} recognizes
a lazy node without traversing its tail.  @code{map} preserves a lazy spine;
@code{take} returns a finite prefix.
Thus @code{listRef(integers(), 17L)} forces only the finite prefix required
by that request.

The representation stores an available head and a memoized tail thunk.
@code{car} reads the head with @code{get(0)}; @code{cdr} forces only the next
tail with @code{drop(1)}.  Operations that explicitly materialize a list,
such as @code{toList} or structural equality, can still traverse an entire
infinite spine and do not terminate.

These prefix selectors let stream equations describe integrations and
differential equations without manually delaying every recursive argument.
The Double helpers below mirror the Long operations,
using the explicit @code{lazyPair} and @code{lazyEnd} constructors.

@example kotlin
fun consDouble(
    x: Double,
    rest: List<Double>,
): List<Double> = lazyPair(x, thunk @{ rest @})

fun carDouble(pair: List<Double>): Double = pair.get(0)

fun cdrDouble(pair: List<Double>): List<Double> = pair.drop(1)

fun listRefDouble(items: List<Double>,
    n: Long): Double =
    if (n == 0L) carDouble(items)
    else listRefDouble(cdrDouble(items), n - 1L)

fun mapDouble(
    proc: (Double) -> Double,
    items: List<Double>,
): List<Double> =
    if (items.isEmpty()) lazyEnd
    else consDouble(
        proc(carDouble(items)),
        mapDouble(proc, cdrDouble(items)),
    )

fun scaleDoubleList(items: List<Double>,
    factor: Double): List<Double> =
    mapDouble(@{ x -> x * factor @}, items)

fun addDoubleLists(
    first: List<Double>,
    second: List<Double>,
): List<Double> =
    if (first.isEmpty()) second
    else if (second.isEmpty()) first
    else consDouble(
        carDouble(first) + carDouble(second),
        addDoubleLists(cdrDouble(first),
            cdrDouble(second)),
    )
@end example

The integrator and solver use the same guest list operations as the examples
above:

@example kotlin
fun integral(integrand: List<Double>,
    initialValue: Double,
    dt: Double): List<Double> @{
    fun int(): List<Double> =
        consDouble(initialValue,
            addDoubleLists(scaleDoubleList(integrand,
                dt),
                int()))
    return int()
@}

fun solve(f: (Double) -> Double, y0: Double,
    dt: Double): List<Double> @{
    fun y(): List<Double> = integral(dy(), y0, dt)
    fun dy(): List<Double> = mapDouble(f, y())
    return y()
@}

fun main() @{
    println(
        listRefDouble(
            solve(@{ x -> x @}, 1.0, 0.001),
            1000L,
        ),
    )
@}
@end example

@noindent
The 1000th Euler step is mathematically
@math{(1.001)^{1000} \approx 2.716923932235896}.  This is the recurrence's
expected value, not an observed engine output.

The resulting lazy list is structured data, not a scalar accepted by the
printer.  Exercise 4.34 asks for explicit bounded string rendering.

